\\)-polynomial counterexamples instead arise from the paper’s direct-sum amplification; no minimal-rank claim is made for those two invariants.\n\n### #Chow polynomials\n\nP. Brändén and L. Vecchi prove real-rootedness of Chow polynomials arising from totally nonnegative matrices and posets. The precise interlacing theorem and its paving-matroid consequence are stated on the matroid page [BV25]. E. Hoster and C. Stump prove real-rootedness of Chow and augmented Chow polynomials for finite graded simplicial posets with a top element whenever the \\(h\\)-vector is positive [HS25]. This includes Cohen–Macaulay simplicial posets and, in particular, lattices of flats of uniform matroids. They also show that the Chow polynomial of the dual poset interlaces the augmented Chow polynomial.\n\nP. Brändén and L. S. M. Leite study the conjecture of C. A. Athanasiadis and A. Kalampogia-Evangelinou that chain polynomials of geometric lattices have only real zeros [BL25]. They prove it for perfect matroid designs, Dowling lattices, and a class containing lattices of flats of paving matroids, and analyze how the property behaves under several lattice operations.\n\nThe three general real-rootedness conjectures for chain, Chow, and augmented Chow polynomials are false. Gergely Berczi, B. Coron, C. Eur, and S. Li construct matroids whose chain polynomials, Chow polynomials, augmented Chow polynomials, and \\(h\\)-polynomials of the order complexes of their proper nonempty flats are not log-concave, and hence not real-rooted [BCEL26, Thms. A–C and Cor. D]. The chain-polynomial examples have rank \\(4,\\) which is minimal because ranks at most \\(3\\) are real-rooted. Their ordinary Chow-polynomial examples have rank \\(7,\\) also minimal because ranks at most \\(6\\) are real-rooted, and may be chosen simple and \\(3\\)-connected. The augmented Chow and \\(h\\)-polynomial counterexamples instead arise from the paper’s direct-sum amplification; no minimal-rank claim is made for those two invariants.\n\nTheorem (Alexandersson, [Ale26, Thms. 1.1 and 4.2]).\n\nLet \\(\\operatorname{Smir}_{n,m}\\) be the words in \\([m]^n\\) whose adjacent letters differ. Then \\[H_{NC_{n+1}}(t)=\\frac{1}{n+1} \\sum_{w\\in\\operatorname{Smir}_{n,n+1}}t^{\\des(w)}\\] is the Chow polynomial of the noncrossing partition lattice \\(NC_{n+1}\\) and is real-rooted for every \\(n\\geq1.\\) More strongly, for fixed \\(n\\) and \\(m\\geq2,\\) the refinements by final letter form an interlacing sequence.\n\nThe proof uses the same last-letter recurrence and matrix interlacing criterion as the word-enumerator argument above.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChow functions for partially ordered sets (https://www.math.sci.hokudai.ac.jp/sympo/fpsac2025/public/slides/vecchi.pdf)\nciteturn3search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Real-rootedness\n\nConjecture (Huh-Stevens, Ferroni–Schröter)\n\n*The Chow polynomial of M only has real roots. ... Proved for\nReal-rootedness\n\nConjecture (Huh-Stevens, Ferroni–Schröter)\n\n*The Chow polynomial of M only has real roots.*\n\nProved for\n- Uniform matroids [Brändén–V.]\n- Matroids of rank at most 5 [Ferroni–Matherne–Stevens–V.]\n\nIf H is non-negative and palindromic\n\nreal-rooted ⇒ γ-positive.\n\nH(t) = ∑ᵢ γᵢ tⁱ (1 + t)^(d−2i)  γ_H(t) = ∑ᵢ γᵢ tⁱ  γᵢ ≥ 0.\n\n21 / 32--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCongreso Bienal de la Real Sociedad Matem´atica Espa˜nola (https://2026.bienalrsme.com/sites/default/files/Matematica_Discreta_y_Algoritmica.pdf)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Alicante, 19 - 23 enero 2026 ... Like the Tutte polynomial, the G-invariant is a matroid invariant, and as\nCongreso Bienal de la Real Sociedad Matem´atica Espa˜nola\nAlicante, 19 - 23 enero 2026\nGraphs with the same G-invariant but diﬀerent conﬁgurations\nAnna de Mier, Joseph E. Bonin, Kerri Morgan and Llu´ıs Vena\nDepartament de Matemem`atiques, Universitat Polit`ecnica de Catalunya\nanna.de.mier@upc.edu\nResumen. In 1974 W. Tutte introduced the rotor construction, that allowed him to construct\n¸codichromatic”graphs (i.e., graphs with the same Tutte polynomial) up to 5-connectivity. We show\nthat the same construction can be applied to yield graphs with the same G-invariant, which is\na valuative matroid invariant that contains, in particular, the Tutte polynomial. This is to our\nknowledge the ﬁrst known construction of such graphs (in contrast, several examples of non-graphic\nmatroids with the same G-invariant are known.) . However, in the case of the G-invariant it applies\nonly for 3-connectivity. Like the Tutte polynomial, the G-invariant is a matroid invariant, and as\nsuch depends only on the cycle structure of the graph. Also as the Tutte polynomial, one does not\nneed the full cycle structure to compute the G-invariant, it is enough to know the ¸conﬁguration.of\nthe corresponding matroid. We show that the graphs we obtain not only have diﬀerent associated\nmatroids but also have diﬀerent conﬁgurations.\nAgradecimientos.\nThis work was partially supported by grant PID2023-147202NB-I00 funded by MI-\nCIU/AEI/10.13039/501100011033.\n9--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Xie_M in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AXie_M+in%3Amath)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; ~Xie}, title = {{N}onnegativity of the $g$-polynomial of split matroids}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2609.35550}, note = {arXiv:2609.35550v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... We prove that the $g$-polynomial of every split matroid has nonnegative coefficients, establishing Speyer's conjecture for a class closed under taking minors and containing all paving and copaving matroids. Our proof uses a deletion--contraction identity obtained by constructing an auxiliary split matroid.\nL. Gao,】 Matthew H. Y. Xie\n\nSep 29 2026 math.CO arXiv:2609.35550v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2609.35550, author = {Alice L.~L.~Gao and Matthew H.~Y.~Xie}, title = {{N}onnegativity of the $g$-polynomial of split matroids}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2609.35550}, note = {arXiv:2609.35550v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe prove that the $g$-polynomial of every split matroid has nonnegative coefficients, establishing Speyer's conjecture for a class closed under taking minors and containing all paving and copaving matroids. Our proof uses a deletion--contraction identity obtained by constructing an auxiliary split matroid. We first show that every simple, cosimple, connected split matroid $M$ has an element $e$ for which both the deletion $M\\setminus e$ and the contraction $M/e$ are connected. More generally, let $M$ be any connected split matroid of rank $k$ on a ground set $E$ with $|E|\\ge4$. Suppose that $e\\in E$ is such that both $M\\setminus e$ and $M/e$ are connected. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n<visual_element id=\"e1\"> (https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/savery/Papers/interval_colourings.pdf)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.5 years ago; It is a longstanding conjecture of Hansen [12] (see also [14, 17]) that such graphs are, in fact, interval colourable. ... ** *Every biregular graph is interval colourable.\n** *It holds that \\(\\theta(n) = n^{o(1)}\\).*\n\nWe proved our upper bound on \\(\\theta(n)\\) by showing that any graph could be edge-decomposed into the desired number of regular bipartite graphs and forests (which are all interval colourable). However, no bound of the form \\(\\theta(n) = o(\\sqrt{n})\\) can be obtained by decomposing only into these two types of graphs, as the following example shows.\n\n**Observation 11.** *Every edge-decomposition of \\(K_{n,\\sqrt{n}}\\) into forests and regular graphs has at least \\((1-o(1))\\sqrt{n}\\) parts.*\n\nThis suggests that to further improve the upper bound on \\(\\theta(n)\\), it might be useful to prove an upper bound on the interval colouring thickness of biregular graphs (where a graph is biregular if it is bipartite and all vertices in the same part have the same degree). It is a longstanding conjecture of Hansen [12] (see also [14, 17]) that such graphs are, in fact, interval colourable.\n\n**Conjecture 12 ([12]).** *Every biregular graph is interval colourable.*\n\nWe pose a weaker conjecture.\n\n**Conjecture 13.** *There exists an absolute constant \\(C\\) such that \\(\\theta(G) \\le C\\) for every biregular graph \\(G\\).*\n\nA proof of Conjecture 13 might pave the way to an improved bound on \\(\\theta(n)\\) via decompositions into forests and biregular graphs. We ask whether this strategy has the potential to prove Conjecture 10.\n\n10--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHoster–Stump Conjecture 1.5: simplicial-poset Chow polynomials | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/research/hoster-stump-2025-chow-polynomials-simplicial-posets/)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Elena Hoster and Christian Stump, Chow polynomials of simplicial posets with positive h-vector are real-rooted, arXiv:2508.15538v1, Section 1, Conjecture 1.5, states: ... The definitions are in Section 1, page 1, equations (1.1)–(1.2); the conjecture is on page 3 and on page 4 of the FPSAC 2026 abstract. ... Each maximal lower interval is a Boolean lattice of rank four, but its Chow polynomial has a non-real complex root.\n\n# Hoster–Stump Conjecture 1.5: simplicial-poset Chow polynomials\n\nLiterature status: not rechecked Repository record: refuted\n\nResolution source: D5/S3/Zeros/SimplicialPosetChowRefutation.resulthttps://arxiv.org/abs/2508.15538v1Source dossierRelease 36a49830636b\n\nProblem\n\n## The question\n\nElena Hoster and Christian Stump, Chow polynomials of simplicial posets with positive h-vector are real-rooted, arXiv:2508.15538v1, Section 1, Conjecture 1.5, states:\n\